<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tavira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tavira is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilao">Gilão</a> river near the Ria Formosa lagoon in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, southern Portugal. Under Muslim rule it was a fortified port and briefly an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> in the 1140s, and in 1242 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> under Paio Peres Correia took it for Portugal. It prospered from fishing and from trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 15th and 16th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tavira.jpg" alt="Tavira" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panarria · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_Tavira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tavira lies in the east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> on the south coast of Portugal, 28 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faro">Faro</a> and 75 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Gilão River meets the Atlantic here. The municipality had 26,167 inhabitants in 2011 and covers 606.97 square kilometres. Tavira is the Portuguese representative community for the inscription of the Mediterranean Diet as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement's origins go back to the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. In the 8th century BCE it became one of the first Phoenician settlements in the west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, with massive walls, at least one sanctuary, two harbours and a regular urban plan, which lasted until the end of the 6th century BCE. Excavations on the hill of Santa Maria found part of a casemate fortification wall and ritual deposits. The site was later revived in the so-called Tartessian period and abandoned again by the end of the 4th century BCE. Under Caesar the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a new port about 7 kilometres from modern Tavira, which prospered and declined with the empire.</p><p>The Moorish period, from the 8th to the 13th century, shaped the area's agriculture, culture and architecture, with whitewashed buildings and Moorish style doors. The Moors built Tavira Castle, two mosques and palaces, and the seven-arched bridge once thought Roman proves to be a 12th-century Moorish work. The area stayed mostly rural until the 11th century, when the town grew rapidly into one of the important settlements of the Gharb al-Andalus. In 1242 Dom Paio Peres Correia retook Tavira after seven of his principal knights were killed during a truce. His troops killed most of the population, and the few survivors were confined to a small quarter known as the Mouraria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the 18th century the river port shipped salt, dried fish and wine, but the earthquake of 1755 destroyed almost all the town's buildings. It was rebuilt with many 18th-century buildings and has 37 churches. The church of Santa Maria do Castelo, built on the ruins of a mosque and dating to the 13th century, holds the tombs of Paio Peres Correia, who died in Tavira in 1275, and his knights. Fishing was once the main industry but declined, partly because of the changing migration of tuna and the silting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilao">Gilão</a>. The sand-bar island of Ilha de Tavira forms part of the Ria Formosa wetlands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tavira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1145, as the Muridun revolt led by Ibn Qasi spread through the Algarve and Almoravid authority collapsed, local leaders took control of <b>Tavira</b>, a port on the Gilão river, and held it independently.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-tavira">Taifa of Tavira</a></li></ul></section>
